import '../tables/cards.drift';
import '../tables/study.drift';

-- Reading the learner's own activity back out of history (UC-12).
--
-- **One statement, one pass, and no new table.** An aggregate table would be a
-- second copy of the truth that five existing write paths — answer, reset, card
-- delete, deck delete, migration — would each have to maintain correctly, and
-- the one that forgets produces a number nothing can check. `study_answers` is
-- append-only (BR-77), so summing it at read time cannot drift from it.
--
-- **Aggregated here, not in Dart.** Two levels of GROUP BY collapse potentially
-- hundreds of thousands of answer rows into one row per local day the user
-- studied. Pulling raw answers up and grouping them in a controller would move
-- the same work into the UI isolate and make the transferred volume grow with
-- history rather than with days.
--
-- **`:utcOffsetSeconds` is a parameter, never a SQLite date function.** Local
-- midnight belongs to `LocalDayModel` and to `utcOffsetProvider` (BR-105,
-- AD-16); a query that derived it from the host would answer differently on the
-- machine that runs the test than on the device, which is the whole reason
-- `:now` is a parameter everywhere else in this project too.
--
-- **Deleted cards need no clause here.** `study_answers.card_id` cascades
-- (`ON DELETE CASCADE`), so a hard-deleted card — directly, or through its deck
-- — takes its history with it and disappears from every day, past ones
-- included (BR-188). Reset is the opposite case and also needs no clause: it
-- never touches `study_answers` (BR-43), so every number here survives it.
--
-- `browse` cannot appear: that stage writes no row at all (BR-111), so it can
-- neither create a card-day nor hold a streak (BR-183).

-- One row per local day that has activity, oldest first. Days with nothing on
-- them are absent, not zero rows — zero-filling the seven-day window is the
-- domain's job (BR-186), and it is the only layer that knows how wide the
-- window is.
--
-- `localDayStart` is the local calendar date expressed as seconds: the instant
-- of that day's local midnight, already shifted by the offset. Read back as
-- `DateTime.fromMillisecondsSinceEpoch(localDayStart * 1000, isUtc: true)` it is
-- `DateTime.utc(y, m, d)` of the local date — byte-for-byte the shape
-- `ProgressActivityDay.localDate` is documented to hold, so the mapper does no
-- calendar arithmetic of its own and cannot disagree with this query about which
-- day a row belongs to.
--
-- **Two GROUP BYs, and both are load-bearing.** The inner one reduces every
-- answer of one card on one day to a single card-day (BR-182); the outer one
-- counts those. Collapsing them into one `COUNT(DISTINCT card_id)` would count
-- the cards correctly and lose the Learning/Reviewing split, because "did this
-- card have a learning answer today" is a property of the card-day, not of any
-- single row.
--
-- `MAX(...)` over the 0/1 flag is a boolean OR: the card-day is Learning if
-- **any** of its answers was `learning`, which is the priority BR-185 states.
-- Reviewing is then the remainder, computed by the domain, so
-- `learning + reviewing = total` holds by construction rather than by two SQL
-- expressions agreeing.
--
-- **`(:utcOffsetSeconds | 0)` is a type pin, and removing it silently breaks
-- the query.** Drift types every arithmetic operator as *real* and propagates
-- that backwards onto the variables in the expression, so the obvious spelling
-- generated `Variable<double>`: SQLite then binds a REAL, the day arithmetic
-- becomes floating-point, and the bucket column comes back as a REAL that
-- `row.read<int>` cannot read. Three other spellings were tried and each was
-- wrong in its own way — `+ 0` still gave `double`; `CAST(:v AS INT)` and
-- `CAST(:v AS INTEGER)` both gave `Variable<String>`, correct only because
-- SQLite coerces the text back, which is not a thing to rely on. Bitwise OR is
-- an integer operator in SQLite, `x | 0` is the identity on integers, and it is
-- the one spelling that makes drift emit `int`. `progress_repository_test.dart`
-- has the test `the day bucket comes back as an integer`, which asserts the
-- column drift hands back. The *parameter* type is held by the call site's
-- signature — `db.progressActivityDays(offset.inSeconds)` will not accept a
-- `double` — so the two together are the guard. Named because this is exactly
-- the kind of line a later cleanup deletes as noise, and somebody who goes
-- looking has to find what is protecting it.
--
-- The day bucket is `localSeconds - (localSeconds % 86400)` rather than a
-- division, for the same reason: `/` is what forced the real typing in the first
-- place. `%` truncates toward zero in SQLite, which would floor the wrong way
-- for a negative operand — impossible here, because `localSeconds` is a unix
-- timestamp shifted by at most fourteen hours and every one of them is far past
-- 1970. Local days are exactly 86400 seconds apart in this app's model, which is
-- what makes the arithmetic agree with `LocalDayModel` exactly: it builds its
-- own boundaries with `DateTime.utc(y, m, d).add(Duration(days: n))`.
--
-- The casts stay out of every WHERE clause, so no index is ever defeated by one.
progressActivityDays:
WITH shifted (localSeconds, cardId, kind) AS (
    SELECT
        CAST(a.answered_at AS INT) + (:utcOffsetSeconds | 0),
        a.card_id,
        a.kind
    FROM study_answers a
),
card_days (localDayStart, cardId, hasLearning) AS (
    SELECT
        s.localSeconds - (s.localSeconds % 86400),
        s.cardId,
        MAX(CASE WHEN s.kind = 'learning' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)
    FROM shifted s
    GROUP BY 1, 2
)
SELECT
    cd.localDayStart AS localDayStart,
    COUNT(*) AS totalCards,
    COALESCE(SUM(cd.hasLearning), 0) AS learningCards
FROM card_days cd
GROUP BY cd.localDayStart
ORDER BY cd.localDayStart ASC;
